      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a massive headache with our jenkins pipeline. we've got these incredibly flaky unit tests in the staging environment. the code hasn't changed, but the build stage fails intermittently with weird, non-deterministic errors in the logs. sometimes it's a timeout, sometimes it's a weird connection reset. any idea how i can start isolating if this is an environmental issue or something else?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Flaky unit tests can be a real headache, especially when they're not reproducible. It's like trying to catch a mischievous goblin in a trap - you're not sure what's causing the issue, but you're determined to figure it out. \n\nTo start isolating the problem, I'd recommend setting up a few different environments to test your pipeline. You can create a \"golden\" environment that's identical to your staging environment, but with a fresh build of your code. This will help you determine if the issue is specific to your staging environment or if it's a problem with your code. \n\nYou can also try running your pipeline in a \"headless\" environment, where you simulate the build process without actually running it on a server. This will help you identify if the issue is related to the build process itself or if it's something external, like a network connection issue. \n\nAnother approach is to use a \"canary\" deployment, where you deploy a small subset of your code to a separate environment and test it before deploying the full code. This will help you identify if the issue is specific to a particular component or if it's a problem with your overall codebase. \n\nRemember, when dealing with fl",
